From: canoziia <canoziia@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] qwen3.8: single-kernel deterministic MoE block alignment

VLLM_QWEN38_FAST_MOE_ALIGN (default 0 = unchanged). With
VLLM_DETERMINISTIC_MOE_ALIGN on, every Marlin MoE layer aligned its routed
entries with ~45 small int64 ATen kernels (stable argsort, scatter_add, two
cumsums, searchsorted, ...): ~80-95 us per layer at decode on CMP 170HX, i.e.
~2.1-2.5 ms per PP stage per decode step for Qwen3.8's 48 MoE layers. One
Triton program now produces exactly the same sorted_ids / expert_ids /
num_tokens_post_pad (entries ordered by (expert, flat index) through a single
bitonic sort of expert<<13|index keys, invalid entries dropped, unused slots =
numel, blocks past the padded total = -1). Integer-only, no atomics, no host
sync, CUDA-graph capturable. 512 experts, block 8: 40 entries 95 -> 7 us,
320 -> 9 us, 1280 -> 22 us. Used only without an expert map and for at most
4096 routed entries; larger (prefill) batches keep the torch path.
